es the map actually reveal intent better? I think yes - it makes the mapping from item type to base premium explicit and declarative. The `?? BASE_PREMIUMS.sword` also makes the default behavior explicit.\n\nOne concern: the map could be seen as over-engineering if there are only 3 types. But given that the test list has more item types coming (potion, rune, moonstone), the map will make future additions trivial - just add a key-value pair. So it also supports future extension.\n\nActually, moonstone base premium is also 30, and rune is 30. Hmm, but we don't have those tests yet. We shouldn't add them prematurely. The test list shows them as todo, so they'll be added in future cycles.\n\nOK, I'm satisfied with this refactor. Let me write the final report.\n\nWait, I should also consider if there's an even simpler refactor. What if I just keep the constants and inline the conditional into a helper? No, the map is better.\n\nWhat about removing the `?? BASE_PREMIUMS.sword` and letting it be implicit? No, that would change behavior for unknown types.\n\nWhat about using a function instead of a map? As discussed, that adds mass. The map is the sweet spot.\n\nLet me finalize.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"## Refactoring Complete\n\n**Refactoring Applied**:\n- Consolidated the item-type-to-base-premium mapping from an `if/else` chain into a declarative `BASE_PREMIUMS` lookup table.\n- Replaced three individual base-premium constants (`SWORD_BASE_PREMIUM`, `AMULET_BASE_PREMIUM`, `STAFF_BASE_PREMIUM`) with one map.\n- Preserved the existing default-to-sword behavior for unknown types via `?? BASE_PREMIUMS.sword`.\n\n**Naming Evaluation**:\n- `processScenario` still fits: it processes a scenario and returns results.\n- `BASE_PREMIUMS` clearly reveals intent: a table of base premiums by item type.\n- No function renames were needed.\n\n**APP Mass Calculation**:\n\n*Before (initial implementation)*:\n```typescript\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n