ntually. But that's test 13. For current test 9, minimal special case works.\n\nI'll implement grouping now actually because it's the right abstraction and not overkill. It handles the current test. The grouping approach: for each item type, if count === 3 and it's a component (rune/moonstone), base = 60, else base * count. Since components have base 25 each. This also handles 3 runes + 3 moonstones.\n\nBut green phase says minimal. I'll implement grouping because it directly satisfies the current test and is clean. It doesn't implement modifiers (future). OK.\n\nImplement:\n```\nconst baseTotal = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + basePremiumFor(item), 0);\n```\nNeed to replace with grouping. Let me write minimal function:\n\n```\nconst componentBaseTotal = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  items.forEach(i => counts.set(i.type, (counts.get(i.type) || 0) + 1));\n  let total = 0;\n  counts.forEach((count, type) => {\n    if (isComponent(type) && count === 3) total += 60;\n    else total += count * (BASE_PREMIUMS[type] ?? BASE_PREMIUMS.sword);\n  });\n  return total;\n};\n```\n\nThis is a bit much but minimal for block rule. Actually maybe simpler:\n\n```\nconst componentPremium = (type: string, count: number): number =>\n  isComponent(type) && count === 3 ? 60 : count * (BASE_PREMIUMS[type] ?? BASE_PREMIUMS.sword);\n```\n\nBut we don't have isComponent yet. We can check base premium === 25 or type is rune/moonstone. Use a COMPONENT_BLOCK_PRICE map.\n\nFor green, let me implement grouping. It passes test 9 and doesn't break others. It's not implementing modifiers. OK.\n\nActually to be strictly minimal, I could do:\n```\nif (items.length === 3 && items.every(i => i.type === \"rune\")) {\n  return [{premium: 65}];\n}\n```\nThis is truly minimal. But then next tests 10-13 will fail and need refactoring. That's fine. But the refactor phase might group. Let me do the minimal special case.\n\nWait, but the skill says \"Implement the minimal code necessary\". The special case is minimal. I'll do that. But it feels dirty. However TDD allows hardcoded returns. OK.\n\nActually, special-casing 3 runes will make test 13 (3 runes + 3 moonstones) fail if I don't also handle moonstones. But that's future. For now minimal special case works.\n\nLet me implement the minimal special case.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.edit:76","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"const buildQuoteFor = (items: Item[]): Quote => {\n  const baseTotal = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + basePremiumFor(item), 0);\n  return [{ premium: baseTotal + PROCESSING_FEE }];\n};","newText":"const buildQuoteFor = (items: Item[]): Quote => {\n  if (items.length === 3 && items.every((item) => item.type === \"rune\")) {\n    return [{ premium: 60 + PROCESSING_FEE }];\n  }\n  const baseTotal = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + basePremiumFor(item), 0);\n  return [{ premium: baseTotal + PROCESSING_FEE }];\n};"}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":1134,"output":1375,"cacheRead":53888,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1187,"totalTokens":56397,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784943733816,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-732886c6-d217-4795-a8fc-f56d3879c421","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.edit:76","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully replaced 1 block(s) in src/claim-office.ts."}],"details":{"diff":"    ...\n 29 const basePremiumFor = (item: Item): number =>\n 30   BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? BASE_PREMIUMS.sword;\n 31 \n 32 const buildQuoteFor = (items: Item[]): Quote => {\n+33   if (items.length === 3 && items.every((item) => item.type === \"rune\")) {\n+34     return [{ premium: 60 + PROCESSING_FEE }];\n+35   }\n 33   const baseTotal = items.reduce((