ly 3 of the same component type gets base premium 60 instead of 75. This is per component type.\n\nGiven that, I could refactor to:\n```typescript\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_BASE_PREMIUM = 60;\n\nconst isComponent = (item: Item): boolean =>\n  item.type === \"rune\" || item.type === \"moonstone\";\n\nconst componentBlockPremium = (items: Item[]): number | null => {\n  if (items.length !== COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE) return null;\n  const type = items[0].type;\n  if (!isComponent(items[0]) || !items.every(item => item.type === type)) return null;\n  return COMPONENT_BLOCK_BASE_PREMIUM;\n};\n\nconst buildQuoteFor = (items: Item[]): Quote => {\n  const blockPremium = componentBlockPremium(items);\n  const baseTotal = blockPremium !== null \n    ? blockPremium \n    : items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + basePremiumFor(item), 0);\n  return [{ premium: baseTotal + PROCESSING_FEE }];\n};\n```\n\nBut wait - this would also apply to 3 moonstones. The test \"should quote exactly 3 runes with block discount\" only tests runes, but \"should apply separate blocks for each component type -- 3 runes + 3 moonstones premium 125 G\" suggests both runes and moonstones get block discounts. So the more general implementation is fine.\n\nHowever, I need to be careful: the current passing tests don't include 3 moonstones. If my refactored code applies the block discount to 3 moonstones too, that's probably fine for future tests, but could it break current tests? Current tests include single moonstone = 30 (passes), and no 3 moonstone test. So it shouldn't break.\n\nBut wait - is this a good refactoring? Let me reconsider. The refactor subagent should make minimal improvements that keep tests green. Generalizing the 3-rune rule to \"3 of any component type\" might be over-engineering if future tests reveal more nuance. However, the spec mentions \"component block rules\" and \"separate blocks for each component type\", so it's reasonable.\n\nActually, let me be more conservative. The safest refactor is:\n1. Extract the magic numbers into named constants\n2. Remove the duplication of `+ PROCESSING_FEE`\n3. Maybe rename/extract the block condition\n\nLet me calculate APP mass before and after.\n\n**Current Code Mass:**\n```typescript\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5; // const 1, binding 1 = 2\n\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: 25,\n  moonstone: 25,\n}; // constants: 6 (values) + 1 object literal? Hmm, object literal is a constant. Let's count each literal as constant: 5,100,60,80,40,25,25 = 7 constants. Plus type annotation? Record<string,number> is syntax. Let's keep it simple: 7 constants = 7. Bindings: BASE_PREMIUMS = 1.\n\ninterface Item { type: string; [key: string]: unknown; } // type definitions have 0 mass\ninterface QuoteStep { ... } // 0\ninterface Scenario { ... } // 0\ntype Quote = Array<{ premium: number }>; // 0\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (item: Item): number =>\n  BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? BASE_PREMIUMS.sword;\n// constants: 0 (no literals)\n// bindings: item = 1\n// invocations: 2 (BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] access? Hmm, property access is not an invocation. BASE_PREMIUMS.sword is property access. So no invocations.)\n// conditionals: 1 (?? nullish coalescing) = 4\n// Total for this function: 1 + 4 = 5\n\nconst buildQuoteFor = (items: Item[]): Quote => {\n  if (items.length === 3 && items.every((item) => item.type === \"rune\")) {\n    return [{ premium: 60 + PROCESSING_FEE }];\n  }\n  const baseTotal = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + basePremiumFor(item), 0);\n  return [{ premium: baseTotal + PROCESSING_FEE }];\n};\n// constants: 3 (3, 60, 0) = 3\n// bindings: items, sum, item, baseTotal = 4\n// invocations: 3 (items.every, items.reduce, basePremiumFor) = 6. Wait, `+` is not an invocation. The arrow function bodies involve no invocations. `items.every` and `items.reduce` are invocations, and `basePremiumFor(item)` is an invocation. So 3 invocations = 6.\n// conditionals: 2 (if, &&) = 8. Actually `&&` is a conditional-like operator. `if` is conditional. So 2 conditionals = 8.\n// loops: 2 (every, reduce) = 10\n// assignments: 1 (baseTotal = ...) = 6\n// Total: 3 + 4 + 6 + 8 + 10 + 6 = 37\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: Scenario): Quote =>\n  buildQuoteFor(scenario.steps[0].items);\n// bindings: scenario = 1\n// invocations: 2 (buildQuoteFor, scenario.steps[0].items - property access? buildQuoteFor is invocation. scenario.steps[0].items is property access chain. Let's not count property access as invocation.)\n// Total: 1 + 2 = 3\n\nOverall total: 2 + 7 + 5 + 37 + 3 = 54\n```\n\nHmm, but constants in BASE_PREMIUMS - I count 7 literal constants. But maybe the object itself is 1 constant and the values are separate? Let me not overthink. The key is to compare before/after consistently.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the APP definition. It says \"Constant (Mass: 1): Literal values (`5`, `\"hello\"`, `true`)\". So each literal value counts. The object `{...}` itself is not a literal value in the same sense. So 7 literals in BASE_PREMIUMS.\n\nLet me focus on a cleaner refactor.\n\n**Refactored Version (conservative):**\n```typescript\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst RUNE_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst RUNE_BLOCK_BASE_PREMIUM = 60;\n\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = { ... };\n\ninterface ... (same)\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (item: Item): number =>\n  BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? BASE_PREMIUMS.sword;\n\nconst isRuneBlock = (items: Item[]): boolean =>\n  items.length === RUNE_BLOCK_SIZE && items.every((item) => item.type === \"rune\");\n\nconst buildQuoteFor = (items: Item[]): Quote => {\n  const baseTotal = isRuneBlock(items)\n    ? RUNE_BLOCK_BASE_PREMIUM\n    : items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + basePremiumFor(item), 0);\n  return [{ premium: baseTotal + PROCESSING_FEE }];\n};\n```\n\nMass for refactored:\n- New constants: RUNE_BLOCK_SIZE = 3, RUNE_BLOCK_BASE_PREMIUM = 60 = 2 constants, 2