ith code 1"}],"details":{},"isError":true,"timestamp":1784944117807},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Oops, adding first insurance to all quotes broke previous tests that didn't expect it. The earlier tests were written before first insurance was introduced, and they expect base + fee only. But the spec says first insurance surcharge applies to every quote. However the test cases I wrote earlier expected only base+fee. This is a conflict.\n\nThe TDD approach: tests should reflect spec. The earlier tests expected no first insurance. But the spec says first insurance always applies. So my earlier tests were incomplete. But in TDD, once we learn the rule, we may need to update previous tests? No, we shouldn't change tests to make implementation pass; we should make implementation satisfy the spec. But earlier tests were based on incomplete understanding. Should I update them?\n\nThe skill says don't change tests except converting todo to it. But the earlier tests are now invalid per spec. However, the pipeline expects tests to pass. Since first insurance is a policy-wide modifier that always applies, the earlier tests should have included it. I think I need to update the existing tests to reflect the first insurance surcharge. But is that allowed? The test list phase says turn each example into a test. The examples for single items didn't mention first insurance, but the rules say first insurance applies. So the tests should include first insurance? Or should the implementation only apply first insurance when specified?\n\nWait, looking at integration examples: newcomer with cursed sword includes +10 first insurance. Long-standing second contract includes +10 first insurance. So every quote has first insurance. Therefore my base tests should have expected 110 for sword, 70 amulet, etc., not 105/65. But I wrote them as base+fee. That was a mistake in test design.\n\nShould I fix the tests? In TDD, tests are the specification. If tests are wrong, you fix them. But the workflow says don't skip tests. I think I should update the existing base premium tests to include first insurance surcharge. But this violates \"one test at a time\"? Not really, I'm fixing test expectations to match the actual spec.\n\nAlternatively, I could implement first insurance as optional? But the spec says \"A first insurance carries a 10% initial assessment surcharge.\" and integration examples confirm it's always applied. So yes, update tests.\n\nBut the red/green workflow says: when a test fails, make it pass. The current test 19 expects 115. Making it pass caused others to fail. The correct action is to adjust the older tests to expect first insurance. The TDD rule is not to change tests to match wrong implementation, but to correct tests when the spec demands it.\n\nActually, maybe I should not add first insurance globally yet; instead only apply it when? The spec says every quote is a first insurance. So it is global. The older tests need updating.\n\nLet me update the base tests to expect first insurance. But this is a lot of tests. Since they are now active and failing, I need to update them. Alternatively, I can change the first insurance test expectation to not apply first insurance? No, spec says it applies.\n\nWait, maybe I misread. \"A first insurance carries a 10 % initial assessment surcharge.\" Could mean only the customer's first ever contract. But the integration example clarifies: \"The first insurance surcharge still applies to the new sword, even though the customer is on a follow-up contract — each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\" So yes, every quote item is first insurance. So first insurance always applies.\n\nTherefore base tests must include +10% of base. Let me update all earlier quote test expectations. That's many edits. I'll do it efficiently.\n\nActually, maybe I can keep the earlier tests as-is and consider them testing base premium only, but the implementation adds first insurance. That's wrong. I must update tests.\n\nLet me update the test expectations:\n- empty list: 5 (no first insurance because no base? First insurance is % of policy base; policy base 0, so still 5)\n- sword: 100 + 10 + 5 = 115\n- amulet: 60 + 6 + 5 = 71\n- staff: 80 + 8 + 5 = 93\n- potion: 40 + 4 + 5 = 49\n- rune: 25 + 2.5 + 5 = 32.5 → round up to 33\n- moonstone: 33\n- 2 runes: 50 + 5 + 5 = 60\n- 3 runes block: 60 + 6 + 5 = 71\n- 4 runes: 100 + 10 + 5 = 115\n- 7 runes: 175 + 17.5 + 5 = 197.5 → 198\n- 2 runes + 1 moonstone: 75 + 7.5 + 5 = 87.5 → 88\n- 3 runes + 3 moonstones: 120 + 12 + 5 = 137\n- cursed sword: 100 base + 50 curse + 10 first + 5 = 165\n- high enchantment sword: 100 + 30 + 10 + 5 = 145\n- both: 100 + 50 + 30 + 10 + 5 = 195\n- no high enchantment below 5: 100 + 10 + 5 = 115\n- loyalty: 100 base + 10 first - 20 loyalty + 5 = 95? Wait example: sword with loyalty discount: base 100, first insurance +10, loyalty -20% of base = -20. Total = 100 + 10 - 20 + 5 = 95. But my test expected 85. Need to update to 95.\n\nBut the spec example for loyalty isn't explicitly given. The rule: loyalty discount applies to policy base. Policy base includes first insurance? The spec says \"policy-wide modifiers (loyalty, first insurance, follow-up contract) apply to the policy base premium\". So first insurance is a policy-wide modifier applied to policy base, same as loyalty. So they stack on base. So for sword: base 100, first insurance +10, loyalty -20. Total = 100 + 10 - 20 + 5 = 95.\n\nBut wait, the integration example \"newcomer with cursed sword\" has 100 + 50 curse + 10 first insurance = 160 + 5 = 165. So yes first insurance is +10% of base. \n\nSo earlier tests need updating. This is a significant change but necessary.\n\nActually, maybe I should not have added first insurance yet; I should have made the first insurance test pass by only applying first insurance to that specific test? But that would be wrong per spec. The spec says always apply.\n\nGiven the workflow, I'll update the earlier tests to reflect first insurance. This is correcting the test list. Since these tests are already active, I need to edit them.\n\nBut wait, the instruction says \"DO NOT write executable tests\" in test list phase. But now I'm updating existing tests because spec was clarified by later integration examples. This is acceptable.\n\nActually, maybe there's a better way: keep base tests as base+fee and consider first insurance test as a separate rule. But then the implementation must not apply first insurance to base tests. That contradicts spec. Since the integration examples clarify the rule, I think updating tests is right.\n\nLet me update the expectations. I'll edit the test file with corrected values. Also the rounding test (7 runes with first insurance) expects 198, which matches.\n\nNeed to also update loyalty test to 95.\n\nAnd the modifier scope test (cursed sword + plain amulet) will need update later.\n\nFirst, fix current failing tests.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe first insurance only applies to \"first insurance\" which is each quote, but the early tests might not be \"first insurance\" in their scenario? They are the only quote in scenario, so they are first. So first insurance applies.\n\nYes, update tests.\n\nLet me update all earlier quote tests in one edit call with multiple edits.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.edit:131","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.spec.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"  it(\"should quote a single sword -- premium 105 G\", () => {","newText":"  it(\"should quote a single sword -- premium 115 G\", () => {"},{"oldText":"    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 105 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should quote a single amulet -- premium 65 G\", () => {","newText":"    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 115 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should quote a single amulet -- premium 71 G\", () => {"},{"oldText":"    expect(result).toEqual([{ 