n3. `index` parameter in `processStep` is used as `isFollowUp` - the name is misleading! It takes an index and uses it as a boolean (`index > 0`). This is a naming/reveals intent issue. It should be named `isFollowUp` or the caller should pass `index > 0`.\n4. `scenario.customer.yearsWithMHPCO` - this is passed as `customerYears` in quoteForItems and basePremiumTotal etc. Fine.\n\nRule 3: No duplication\n- The `reimbursementForDamage` has a magic `0.5` duplicated conceptually with the halving logic. We have `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.3` but damage halving uses `0.5` inline.\n- The threshold `8` in `reimbursementForDamage` vs `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5` - these are different thresholds. Could extract `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD = 8`.\n- `BASE_PREMIUMS` and `INSURANCE_VALUES` have parallel structures - maybe not duplication.\n- `typeof item.enchantment === \"number\"` check appears twice (surchargeForItem and reimbursementForDamage) - could extract a helper but might not be worth it.\n\nRule 4: Fewest elements\n- Could remove `cause` from incident since it's not used? But it's part of the data structure and might be used in future tests.\n- The `material` field isn't used in quote logic except possibly dragon material in claims. Wait, the dragon material test passes, but looking at code, `reimbursementForDamage` doesn't check material at all. It only checks enchantment >= 8. The test for dragon material shows payout 700 for damage 800, which is 800 - 100 = 700. That's the same as regular damage. So material is not used. But future tests might use it.\n\nWait, looking more carefully at tests:\n- \"should fully reimburse dragon-material damage minus deductible -- payout 700 G\" - currently passes because 800 - 100 = 700. No special dragon logic.\n- \"should halve damage for enchantment level 9 or higher -- payout 400 G\" - 1000 * 0.5 - 100 = 400. Passes.\n- todo: \"should apply enchantment clause over dragon material when both apply -- payout 400 G\" - this suggests dragon material should have a special clause. So material handling will be added later.\n\nLet me focus on safe refactorings:\n1. Rename `index` parameter in `processStep` to `isFollowUp` and update call site to pass `index > 0`. Wait, `processStep` uses `index` to check `index > 0`. But the caller `processScenario` passes the actual index. If I rename parameter to `isFollowUp`, the caller should pass `index > 0`. This reveals intent better.\n\n2. Extract constants for high enchantment damage threshold (8) and halving rate (0.5).\n\n3. Rename `valueForItem` to `insuredValueFor` to better reveal intent.\n\nLet me calculate APP mass before and after.\n\nCurrent code mass calculation:\nActually, let me be careful with counting. I'll count the implementation only.\n\nCurrent code:\n```\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;                                  // constant 5\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;                            // constant 3\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;                        // constant 60\nconst CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.5;                         // constant 0.5\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.3;               // constant 0.3\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;                      // constant 5\n\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.2;                         // constant 0.2\nconst LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD = 2;                         // constant 2\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;                // constant 0.1\nconst FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;                      // constant 0.15\nconst DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE = 100;                             // constant 100\nconst CAP_MULTIPLIER = 2;                                  // constant 2\n\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = { ... };     // constants inside object: 6\nconst INSURANCE_VALUES: Record<string, number> = { ... };  // constants inside object: 6\n\n// Type declarations don't count toward mass typically (they're compile-time)\n// Interfaces don't count\n\nconst isComponent = (type: string): boolean => type === \"rune\" || type === \"moonstone\";\n// constants: \"rune\", \"moonstone\" = 2\n// bindings: type = 1\n// conditionals: || = 1? Actually || is a conditional operator. But usually we don't count boolean operators as conditionals for APP? The APP spec says \"conditionals: if, switch, ?:\". Logical operators are technically conditionals in some counting schemes. I'll be conservative and not count || and && as conditionals.\n\nconst basePremiumForItem = (type: string): number =>\n  BASE_PREMIUMS[type] ?? BASE_PREMIUMS.sword;\n// bindings: type = 1\n// invocations: BASE_PREMIUMS... property access not really invocation. The ?? operator is conditional. = 1 conditional.\n\nconst basePremiumForGroup = (type: string, count: number): number =>\n  isComponent(type) && count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE\n    ? COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM\n    : count * basePremiumForItem(type);\n// bindings: type, count = 2\n// invocations: isComponent(type), basePremiumForItem(type) = 2\n// conditionals: && (conditional?), ?: = 2 if counting &&. Let's count ?: as 1, && as 1 = 2.\n\nconst surchargeWhen = (applies: boolean, base: number, rate: number): number =>\n  applies ? base * rate : 0;\n// bindings: applies, base, rate = 3\n// conditionals: ?: = 1\n// constants: 0 = 1\n\nconst surchargeForItem = (item: Item): number => {\n  const base = basePremiumForItem(item.type);\n  return (\n    surchargeWhen(item.cursed ?? false, base, CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE) +\n    surchargeWhen(\n      typeof item.enchantment === \"number\" && item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD,\n      base,\n      HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE\n    )\n  );\n};\n// bindings: item, base = 2\n// invocations: basePremiumForItem(item.type), surchargeWhen(...), surchargeWhen(...) = 3\n// conditionals: ??, &&, >=, ?: (inside surchargeWhen called twice but we count call sites? No, we count in the function body). Actually conditionals in this function body: ?? (1), && (1), >= (1) = 3. The ?: are in surchargeWhen body, counted there.\n// constants: false = 1\n\nconst valueForItem = (type: string): number =>\n  INSURANCE_VALUES[type] ?? INSURANCE_VALUES.sword;\n// bindings: type = 1\n// conditionals: ?? = 1\n\nconst insuranceSumForItems = (items: Item[]): number =>\n  items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + valueForItem(item.type), 0);\n// bindings: items, sum, item = 3\n// invocations: reduce (1), valueForItem (1), + (1) = 3\n// loops: reduce = 1\n// constants: 0 = 1\n\nconst basePremiumTotal = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  const quantityByType = new Map<string, number>();\n  items.forEach((item) => {\n    quantityByType.set(item.type, (quantityByType.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  });\n\n  return Array.from(quantityByType.entries()).reduce(\n    (total, [type, count]) => total + basePremiumForGroup(type, count),\n    0\n  );\n};\n// bindings: items, quantityByType, item, total, type, count = 6\n// invocations: Map constructor (1), set (1), get (1), Array.from (1), reduce (2), basePremiumForGroup (1), + (2) = 9\n// loops: forEach (1), reduce (1) = 2\n// conditionals: ?? = 1\n// assignments: set is an assignment/mutation = 1\n// constants: 0 (initial), 0 (reduce), 1 = 3\n\nconst calculatePolicyAdjustment = (\n  baseTotal: number,\n  customerYears: number,\n  isFollowUp: boolean\n): number => {\n  const loyaltyDiscount =\n    customerYears >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD\n      ? LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE * baseTotal\n      : 0;\n\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE * baseTotal;\n  const followUpDiscount = isFollowUp ? FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE * baseTotal : 0;\n\n  return -loyaltyDiscount + firstInsuranceSurcharge - followUpDiscount;\n};\n// bindings: baseTotal, customerYears, isFollowUp, loyaltyDiscount, firstInsuranceSurcharge, followUpDiscount = 6\n// conditionals: >= (1), ?: (2) = 3\n// constants: 0 = 1\n\nconst quoteForItems = (\n  items: Item[],\n  customerYears: number,\n  isFollowUp: boolean\n): { premium: number } => {\n  const baseTotal = basePremiumTotal(items);\n\n  const itemSurcharges = items.reduce(\n    (total, item) => total + surchargeForItem(item),\n    0\n  );\n\n  const policyBase = baseTotal + itemSurcharges;\n  const rawPremium =\n    policyBase + calculatePolicyAdjustment(baseTotal, customerYears, isFollowUp) + PROCESSING_FEE;\n  return { premium: Math.ceil(rawPremium) };\n};\n// bindings: items, customerYears, isFollowUp, baseTotal, total, item, policyBase, rawPremium = 8\n// invocations: basePremiumTotal (1), reduce (1), surchargeForItem (1), + (4), calculatePolicyAdjustment (1), Math.ceil (1) = 10\n// loops: reduce = 1\n// constants: 0 = 1\n\nconst reimbursementForDamage = (policyItem: Item, damageAmount: number): number => {\n  const enchantment = typeof policyItem.enchantment === \"number\" ? policyItem.enchantment : 0;\n  const amountAfterClauses =\n    enchantment >= 8 ? damageAmount * 0.5 : damageAmount;\n  return Math.max(0, amountAfterClauses - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE);\n};\n// bindings: policyItem, damageAmount, enchantment, amountAfterClauses = 4\n// conditionals: typeof === (1), ?:(1), >= (1), ?:(1) = 4\n// invocations: Math.max (1), - (1), * (1) = 3\n// constants: 0 (twice?), 0.5, 8, DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE constant ref = 4? Actually literal constants: 0 (two occurrences), 0.5, 8 = 4\n\nconst processClaim = (\n  policyItems: Item[],\n  damages: Array<{ itemType: string; amount: number }>\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } => {\n  const cap = CAP_MULTIPLIER * insuranceSumForItems(policyItems);\n  const payoutBeforeCap = damages.reduce((sum, damage) => {\n    const policyItem = policyItems.find((item) => item.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (!policyItem) {\n      throw new Error(`Item type ${damage.itemType} not insured`);\n    }\n    return sum + reimbursementForDamage(policyItem, damage.amount);\n  }, 0);\n  const payout = Math.min(payoutBeforeCap, cap);\n  return { payout: Math.floor(payout), remainingCap: Math.floor(cap - payout) };\n};\n//