ests Pass:** All 35 passing tests still pass; 4 `it.todo` tests remain untouched.\n- **Rule 2 — Reveals Intent:** `damageAfterEnchantmentReduction` and `isFollowUp` make the code's purpose clearer.\n- **Rule 3 — No Duplication:** Reusing `surchargeWhen` removes the duplicated conditional-multiplication pattern.\n- **Rule 4 — Fewest Elements:** Only one existing helper was reused; no new abstractions were added.\n\n## Test Result\n\n```text\n✓ src/claim-office.spec.ts  (39 tests | 4 skipped)\nTest Files  1 passed (1)\nTests  35 passed | 4 todo (39)\n```"}],"details":{"mode":"single","agentScope":"both","projectAgentsDir":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-35-59_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_kimi-k2-7/.pi/agents","results":[{"agent":"refactor","agentSource":"project","task":"Test file: src/claim-office.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/claim-office.ts\nPassing tests: 35\nRecent Green phase: Fixed payout rounding so remainingCap is reduced by the floored payout.\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green. The project uses TypeScript and Vitest; run `pnpm test` to verify.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done.","exitCode":0,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Task: Test file: src/claim-office.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/claim-office.ts\nPassing tests: 35\nRecent Green phase: Fixed payout rounding so remainingCap is reduced by the floored payout.\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green. The project uses TypeScript and Vitest; run `pnpm test` to verify.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done."}],"timestamp":1784944581623},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to refactor the implementation in src/claim-office.ts while keeping all tests green. I need to:\n1. First read the implementation and test files\n2. Evaluate naming\n3. Calculate APP mass before\n4. Apply simple design rules\n5. Implement refactoring\n6. Run tests\n7. Calculate APP mass after\n8. Document decisions\n\nLet me start by reading both files.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.read:0","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.read:1","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.spec.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":84,"output":122,"cacheRead":6016,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":84,"totalTokens":6222,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784944581645,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-7caf1927-dd7d-42ae-94c8-0101af0e437f","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.read:0","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"const PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\nconst CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.3;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD = 8;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_REDUCTION_RATE = 0.5;\n\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.2;\nconst LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD = 2;\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;\nconst DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\nconst CAP_MULTIPLIER = 2;\n\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: 25,\n  moonstone: 25,\n};\n\nconst INSURANCE_VALUES: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 1000,\n  amulet: 600,\n  staff: 800,\n  potion: 400,\n  rune: 250,\n  moonstone: 250,\n};\n\ninterface Item {\n  type: string;\n  cursed?: boolean;\n  enchantment?: number;\n  material?: string;\n  damage?: number;\n}\n\ninterface QuoteStep {\n  op: \"quote\";\n  items: Item[];\n}\n\ninterface ClaimStep {\n  op: \"claim\";\n  policy: number;\n  incident: {\n    cause: string;\n    damages: Array<{ itemType: string; amount: number }>;\n  };\n}\n\ntype Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\n\ninterface Scenario {\n  customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number };\n  steps: Step[];\n}\n\nconst isComponent = (type: string): boolean => type === \"rune\" || type === \"moonstone\";\n\nconst basePremiumForItem = (type: string): number =>\n  BASE_PREMIUMS[type] ?? BASE_PREMIUMS.sword;\n\nconst basePremiumForGroup = (type: string, count: number): number =>\n  isComponent(type) && count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE\n    ? COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM\n    : count * basePremiumForItem(type);\n\nconst surchargeWhen = (applies: boolean, base: number, rate: number): number =>\n  applies ? base * rate : 0;\n\nconst surchargeForItem = (item: Item): number => {\n  const base = basePremiumForItem(item.type);\n  return (\n    surchargeWhen(item.cursed ?? false, base, CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE) +\n    surchargeWhen(\n      typeof item.enchantment === \"number\" && item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD,\n      base,\n      HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE\n    )\n  );\n};\n\nconst insuredValueFor = (type: string): number =>\n  INSURANCE_VALUES[type] ?? INSURANCE_VALUES.sword;\n\nconst insuranceSumForItems = (items: Item[]): number =>\n  items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + insuredValueFor(item.type), 0);\n\nconst basePremiumTotal = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  const quantityByType = new Map<string, number>();\n  items.forEach((item) => {\n    quantityByType.set(item.type, (quantityByType.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  });\n\n  return Array.from(quantityByType.entries()).reduce(\n    (total, [type, count]) => total + basePremiumForGroup(type, count),\n    0\n  );\n};\n\nconst policyAdjustmentAmount = (\n  baseTotal: number,\n  customerYears: number,\n  isFollowUp: boolean\n): number => {\n  const loyaltyDiscount =\n    customerYears >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD\n      ? LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE * baseTotal\n      : 0;\n\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE * baseTotal;\n  const followUpDiscount = isFollowUp ? FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE * baseTotal : 0;\n\n  return firstInsuranceSurcharge - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount;\n};\n\nconst quoteForItems = (\n  items: Item[],\n  customerYears: number,\n  isFollowUp: boolean\n): { premium: number } => {\n  const baseTotal = basePremiumTotal(items);\n\n  const itemSurcharges = items.reduce(\n    (total, item) => total + surchargeForItem(item),\n    0\n  );\n\n  const policyBase = baseTotal + itemSurcharges;\n  const rawPremium =\n    policyBase + policyAdjustmentAmount(baseTotal, customerYears, isFollowUp) + PROCESSING_FEE;\n  return { premium: Math.ceil(rawPremium) };\n};\n\nconst reimbursementForDamage = (policyItem: Item, damageAmount: number): number => {\n  const enchantment = typeof policyItem.enchantment === \"number\" ? policyItem.enchantment : 0;\n  const amountAfterClauses =\n    enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD\n      ? damageAmount * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_REDUCTION_RATE\n      : damageAmount;\n  return Math.max(0, amountAfterClauses - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE);\n};\n\nconst capForPolicy = (items: Item[]): number =>\n  CAP_MULTIPLIER * insuranceSumForItems(items);\n\nconst quoteStepForPolicy = (steps: Step[], policyIndex: number): QuoteStep => {\n  const step = steps[policyIndex];\n  if (step.op !== \"quote\") {\n    throw new Error(`Policy ${policyIndex} is not a quote step`);\n  }\n  return step;\n};\n\nconst processClaim = (\n  policyItems: Item[],\n  damages: Array<{ itemType: string; amount: number }>,\n  remainingCap: number\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } => {\n  const payoutBeforeCap = damages.reduce((sum, damage) => {\n    const policyItem = policyItems.find((item) => item.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (!policyItem) {\n      throw new Error(`Item type ${damage.itemType} not insured`);\n    }\n    return sum + reimbursementForDamage(policyItem, damage.amount);\n  }, 0);\n  const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(payoutBeforeCap, remainingCap));\n  return { payout, remainingCap: Math.floor(remainingCap - payout) };\n};\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: Scenario): Array<{ premium: number } | { payout: number; remainingCap: number }> => {\n  const remainingCapByPolicy = new Map<number, number>();\n  return scenario.steps.map((step, index) => {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      return quoteForItems(step.items, scenario.customer.yearsWithMHPCO, index > 0);\n    }\n    const policyStep = quoteStepForPolicy(scenario.steps, step.policy);\n    const cap = capForPolicy(policyStep.items);\n    const remainingCap = remainingCapByPolicy.get(step.policy) ?? cap;\n    const result = processClaim(policyStep.items, step.incident.damages, remainingCap);\n    remainingCapByPolicy.set(step.policy, result.remainingCap);\n    return result;\n  });\n};\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784944582868},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.read:1","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { processScenario } from \"./claim-office.js\";\n\ndescribe(\"MHPCO Claim Office\", () => {\n  // Basic quote premiums\n  it(\"should return only the processing fee for an empty item list -- premium 5 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [{ op: \"quote\", items: [] }],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 5 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should quote a single sword -- premium 115 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 115 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should quote a single amulet -- premium 71 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"amulet\", material: \"silver\", enchantment: 2, cursed: false }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 71 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should quote a single staff -- premium 93 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"staff\", material: \"oak\", enchantment: 1, cursed: false }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 93 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should quote a single potion -- premium 49 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"potion\", material: \"glass\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 49 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should quote a single rune -- premium 33 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"rune\" }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 33 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should quote a single moonstone -- premium 33 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"moonstone\" }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 33 }]);\n  });\n\n  // Component block rules\n  it(\"should quote 2 runes without block discount -- premium 60 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"rune\" }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 60 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should quote exactly 3 runes with block discount -- premium 71 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"rune\" }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 71 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should quote 4 runes without block discount -- premium 115 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"rune\" }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 115 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should quote 7 runes without block discount -- premium 198 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: Array.from({ length: 7 }, () => ({ type: \"rune\" })),\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 198 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should not mix different component types for the block discount -- 2 runes + 1 moonstone premium 88 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"moonstone\" }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 88 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should apply separate blocks for each component type -- 3 runes + 3 moonstones premium 137 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [\n            { type: \"rune\" },\n            { type: \"rune\" },\n            { type: \"rune\" },\n            { type: \"moonstone\" },\n            { type: \"moonstone\" },\n            { type: \"moonstone\" },\n          ],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 137 }]);\n  });\n\n  // Item-specific modifiers\n  it(\"should add cursed surcharge to a sword -- premium 165 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: true }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 165 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should add high-enchantment surcharge at level 5 -- premium 145 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 5, cursed: false }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 145 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should add both cursed and high-enchantment surcharges -- premium 195 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 5, cursed: true }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 195 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should not apply high-enchantment surcharge below level 5 -- premium 115 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 4, cursed: false }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 115 }]);\n  });\n\n  // Policy-wide modifiers\n  it(\"should apply loyalty discount at exactly 2 years -- premium 95 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 2 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 95 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should apply first insurance surcharge -- premium 115 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 115 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should apply follow-up contract discount on the second quote -- premium 100 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false }],\n        },\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 115 }, { premium: 100 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should apply item-specific modifiers per item and policy-wide modifiers on policy base -- cursed sword + plain amulet premium 231 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [\n            { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: true },\n            { type: \"amulet\", material: \"silver\", enchantment: 2, cursed: false },\n          ],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 231 }]);\n  });\n\n  // Integration examples\n  it(\"should compute newcomer with a cursed sword -- premium 165 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: true }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 165 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should compute long-standing customer's second contract -- premium 160 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 3 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false }],\n        },\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 7, cursed: true }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 95 }, { premium: 160 }]);\n  });\n\n  // Claim standard reimbursement\n  it(\"should reimburse a regular steel sword damage minus deductible -- payout 400 G, remaining cap 1600 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false }],\n        },\n        {\n          op: \"claim\",\n          policy: 0,\n          incident: {\n            cause: \"fire\",\n            damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 }],\n          },\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 115 }, { payout: 400, remainingCap: 1600 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should reimburse a rune damage minus deductible -- payout 100 G, remaining cap 400 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"rune\" }],\n        },\n        {\n          op: \"claim\",\n          policy: 0,\n          incident: {\n            cause: \"fire\",\n            damages: [{ itemType: \"rune\", amount: 200 }],\n          },\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 33 }, { payout: 100, remainingCap: 400 }]);\n  });\n\n  // Claim special clauses\n  it(\"should fully reimburse dragon-material damage minus deductible -- payout 700 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"dragon\", enchantment: 5, cursed: false }],\n        },\n        {\n          op: \"claim\",\n          policy: 0,\n          incident: {\n            cause: \"dragon attack\",\n            damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 800 }],\n          },\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 145 }, { payout: 700, remainingCap: 1300 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should halve damage for enchantment level 9 or higher -- payout 400 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 9, cursed: false }],\n        },\n        {\n          op: \"claim\",\n          policy: 0,\n          incident: {\n            cause: \"magic surge\",\n            damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1000 }],\n          },\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 145 }, { payout: 400, remainingCap: 1600 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should apply enchantment clause over dragon material when both apply -- payout 400 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"dragon\", enchantment: 9, cursed: false }],\n        },\n        {\n          op: \"claim\",\n          policy: 0,\n          incident: {\n            cause: \"dragon attack\",\n            damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1000 }],\n          },\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 145 }, { payout: 400, remainingCap: 1600 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should apply dragon-material clause when only it applies -- payout 700 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"dragon\", enchantment: 5, cursed: false }],\n        },\n        {\n          op: \"claim\",\n          policy: 0,\n          incident: {\n            cause: \"dragon attack\",\n            damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 800 }],\n          },\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 145 }, { payout: 700, remainingCap: 1300 }]);\n  });\n\n  // Multi-item / deductible\n  it(\"should apply deductible per damaged item in a single incident -- total payout 600 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [\n            { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false },\n            { type: \"amulet\", material: \"silver\", enchantment: 2, cursed: false },\n          ],\n        },\n        {\n          op: \"claim\",\n          policy: 0,\n          incident: {\n            cause: \"dragon attack\",\n            damages: [\n              { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 },\n              { itemType: \"amulet\", amount: 300 },\n            ],\n          },\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([\n      { premium: 181 },\n      { payout: 600, remainingCap: 2600 },\n    ]);\n  });\n  it(\"should sum insurance values for multiple items of the same type -- cap 4000 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [\n            { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false },\n            { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false },\n          ],\n        },\n        {\n          op: \"claim\",\n          policy: 0,\n          incident: {\n            cause: \"dragon attack\",\n            damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 100 }],\n          },\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 225 }, { payout: 0, remainingCap: 4000 }]);\n  });\n\n  // Cap exhaustion\n  it(\"should cap the first claim and track remaining cap -- first payout 1400 G, remaining 600 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false }],\n        },\n        {\n          op: \"claim\",\n          policy: 0,\n          incident: {\n            cause: \"fire\",\n            damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1500 }],\n          },\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 115 }, { payout: 1400, remainingCap: 600 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should reduce the second claim by remaining cap -- second payout 600 G, remaining 0 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false }],\n        },\n        {\n          op: \"claim\",\n          policy: 0,\n          incident: {\n            cause: \"fire\",\n            damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1500 }],\n          },\n        },\n        {\n          op: \"claim\",\n          policy: 0,\n          incident: {\n            cause: \"fire\",\n            damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1500 }],\n          },\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([\n      { premium: 115 },\n      { payout: 1400, remainingCap: 600 },\n      { payout: 600, remainingCap: 0 },\n    ]);\n  });\n\n  // Rounding\n  it(\"should round final premium up in MHPCO's favor -- 7 runes with first insurance yields 198 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: Array.from({ length: 7 }, () => ({ type: \"rune\" })),\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 198 }]);\n  });\n  it(\"should round final payout down in MHPCO's favor -- high-enchantment damage 901 G yields 350 G\", () => {\n    const result = processScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 9, cursed: false }],\n        },\n        {\n          op: \"claim\",\n          policy: 0,\n          incident: {\n            cause: \"magic surge\",\n            damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 901 }],\n          },\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result).toEqual([{ premium: 145 }, { payout: 350, remainingCap: 1650 }]);\n  });\n\n  // Error cases\n  it.todo(\"should reject unknown item type in quote\");\n  it.todo(\"should reject unknown item type in claim\");\n  it.todo(\"should reject negative damage amount\");\n  it.todo(\"should reject more damages than insured items\");\n});\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784944582868},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the full picture. Let me run tests first to ensure they pass before refactoring.\n\nThen I need to evaluate naming and apply simple design rules. Let me think about possible improvements:\n\n1. **Naming evaluation**:\n   - `processScenario` - good, it processes a scenario\n   - `isComponent` - clear\n   - `basePremiumForItem` - clear\n   - `basePremiumForGroup` - clear\n   - `surchargeWhen` - clear\n   - `surchargeForItem` - clear\n   - `insuredValueFor` - clear\n   - `insuranceSumForItems` - clear\n   - `basePremiumTotal` - clear\n   - `policyAdjustmentAmount` - clear\n   - `quoteForItems` - clear\n   - `reimbursementForDamage` - clear\n   - `capForPolicy` - clear\n   - `quoteStepForPolicy` - clear\n   - `processClaim` - clear\n\n2. **Potential improvements**:\n   - In `processScenario`, the `index > 0` check for follow-up is a bit implicit. Could extract `isFollowUpPolicy(steps, stepIndex)` helper.\n   - In `quoteForItems`, `policyBase` and `rawPremium` calculation could be clearer. The name `policyBase` is actually `baseTotal + itemSurcharges`, which is fine.\n   - In `processClaim`, the `remainingCap: Math.floor(remainingCap - payout)` duplicates flooring. We could extract but maybe not necessary.\n   - In `basePremiumTotal`, using `Map` and `Array.from` is fine.\n   - In `reimbursementForDamage`, the `amountAfterClauses` name doesn't really represent clauses. It represents the damage after enchantment reduction. The tests mention \"dragon-material\" clause but looking at the code, I don't see any dragon material handling! Wait, let me re-read... \n\nActually, looking at the tests:\n- \"should fully reimburse dragon-material damage minus deductible -- payout 700 G\"\n- \"should halve damage for enchantment level 9 or higher -- payout 400 G\"\n- \"should apply enchantment clause over dragon material when both apply -- payout 400 G\"\n\nBut in the implementation `reimbursementForDamage`, there's NO dragon material logic! It only halves damage for enchantment >= 8. But the tests are passing... Let me check the test values.\n\nTest: dragon material, enchantment 5, amount 800, payout 700.\nreimbursementForDamage: enchantment=5, amountAfterClauses = 800, return max(0, 800-100) = 700. OK.\n\nTest: enchantment 9, amount 1000, payout 400.\nreimbursementForDamage: enchantment=9, amountAfterClauses = 1000 * 0.5 = 500, return max(0, 500-100) = 400. OK.\n\nTest: dragon material + enchantment 9, amount 1000, payout 400.\nSame as above, enchantment clause dominates. OK.\n\nTest: dragon material only, enchantment 5, amount 800, payout 700.\nNo enchantment reduction, so 700. OK.\n\nSo the tests don't actually test a separate dragon material clause. The implementation is minimal and tests pass. But the naming `amountAfterClauses` is misleading since there's only one clause (enchantment reduction). It could be renamed to `damageAfterEnchantmentReduction`.\n\nActually wait, is there a missing dragon material clause? The tests say \"dragon-material clause\" but the implementation doesn't have it. Since all tests pass, I shouldn't add new behavior. But I can improve naming.\n\nLet me think of concrete refactorings:\n\n1. **Naming improvement**: Rename `amountAfterClauses` to `damageAfterEnchantmentReduction` in `reimbursementForDamage` - this better reveals intent.\n\n2. **Extract helper**: In `processScenario`, `index > 0` could be extracted to `isFollowUpStep(index)` or similar, but it's trivial.\n\n3. **Remove duplication**: In `processClaim`, the `Math.floor` is applied to both payout and remainingCap. Could extract a helper, but might not be worth it.\n\n4. **Clarity**: In `basePremiumTotal`, we can use `for...of` instead of `forEach` to reduce mass slightly, but it's already clear.\n\nLet me think about APP mass calculation first.\n\nFor the current code:\n\nConstants (literal values):\n- 5, 3, 60, 0.5, 0.3, 5, 8, 0.5, 0.2, 2, 0.1, 0.15, 100, 2 = 14 constants\n- Object literal values: sword:1000, amulet:600, staff:800, potion:400, rune:250, moonstone:250, sword:100, amulet:60, staff:80, potion:40, rune:25, moonstone:25 = 24 constants\n- 0 in reduce, 0 in ??, false, 0 in typeof checks, etc.\n\nThis is getting complex. Let me count more systematically by reading the file.\n\nActually, I think the best refactoring is:\n1. Rename `amountAfterClauses` to `damageAfterEnchantmentReduction` for clarity.\n2. Extract `isFollowUpStep(index)` helper to make `index > 0` intent clearer.\n\nBut let me check if there's a better one. Let me look at `quoteForItems`:\n```\nconst policyBase = baseTotal + itemSurcharges;\nconst rawPremium =\n    policyBase + policyAdjustmentAmount(baseTotal, customerYears, isFollowUp) + PROCESSING_FEE;\n```\n\nThis is clear. Could inline `policyBase` but that would reduce clarity.\n\nLet me look at `processClaim`:\n```\nconst payoutBeforeCap = damages.reduce((sum, damage) => {\n    const policyItem = policyItems.find((item) => item.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (!policyItem) {\n      throw new Error(`Item type ${damage.itemType} not insured`);\n    }\n    return sum + reimbursementForDamage(policyItem, damage.amount);\n  }, 0);\nconst payout = Math.floor(Math.min(payoutBeforeCap, remainingCap));\nreturn { payout, remainingCap: Math.floor(remainingCap - payout) };\n```\n\nHere `Math.floor` appears twice. The intent is: payout is floored, and remainingCap is floored too. But actually since remainingCap is already an integer passed in, and payout is floored, `remainingCap - payout` is already an integer. Wait, but remainingCap could be a non-integer? It starts as `capForPolicy` which is `CAP_MULTIPLIER * insuranceSumForItems(items)`, and insuranceSumForItems sums INSURANCE_VALUES, so it's an integer. Then after first claim, remainingCap is `Math.floor(remainingCap - payout)`, so it's an integer. So the second Math.floor is redundant! But it was just added in the recent green phase. We should NOT remove it because tests pass and it might be there for safety, but actually it doesn't change behavior.\n\nHmm, but removing redundant Math.floor would be a valid refactoring since the value is already integer. But the recent green phase explicitly added it. Maybe we should leave it for now.\n\nActually, the task says \"Fixed payout rounding so remainingCap is reduced by the floored payout.\" So they want remainingCap to be reduced by the floored payout, not by the unrounded payout. The current code does:\n```\nconst payout = Math.floor(Math.min(payoutBeforeCap, remainingCap));\nreturn { payout, remainingCap: Math.floor(remainingCap - payout) };\n```\nSince payout is already floored, and remainingCap is integer, Math.floor(remainingCap - payout) = remainingCap - payout. The Math.floor around the subtraction is redundant but documents intent. We could keep it or remove it. Removing it would reduce mass by 1 invocation. But maybe clarity is better served by keeping it? Actually, the expression `remainingCap - payout` is already clearly integer subtraction. The extra Math.floor adds noise.\n\nLet me consider a cleaner refactoring. What if we extract a helper:\n```\nconst floored = (value: number): number => Math.floor(value);\n```\nThen use it. But that adds abstraction. Not necessarily better.\n\nWhat about this refactoring in `processClaim`:\n```\nconst payout = Math.floor(Math.min(payoutBeforeCap, remainingCap));\nreturn { payout, remainingCap: remainingCap - payout };\n```\nThis removes redundant Math.floor. But is it safe? Since remainingCap is always integer (as argued), yes. But if future changes make remainingCap non-integer, this could break. However, the tests would catch that.\n\nWait, but the recent green phase explicitly added `Math.floor(remainingCap - payout)`. Removing it might be seen as undoing the fix. Let me think about whether the tests would still pass. Since remainingCap is integer, yes. But the intent of the recent green phase was to reduce remainingCap by the floored payout. With payout floored, `remainingCap - payout` already achieves that. The Math.floor wrapper was perhaps defensive.\n\nI think a safer refactoring is naming improvement. Let me identify the best naming improvement.\n\nIn `processScenario`:\n```javascript\nreturn scenario.steps.map((step, index) => {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      return quoteForItems(step.items, scenario.customer.yearsWithMHPCO, index > 0);\n    }\n```\nThe expression `index > 0` doesn't clearly express \"is this a follow-up contract?\" A helper `isFollowUpStep(steps, index)` or just `index > 0` could be extracted to `const isFollowUp = index > 0;`.\n\nActually, a better name would be: `const isFollowUpPolicy = index > 0;` before the quoteForItems call.\n\nBut even better: extract a helper function `isFollowUpStep(stepIndex: number): boolean` but it just checks `stepIndex > 0`. Hmm, maybe not worth a function.\n\nWhat about extracting `policyNumber` variable? No.\n\nLet me look at `quoteStepForPolicy`:\n```javascript\nconst quoteStepForPolicy = (steps: Step[], policyIndex: number): QuoteStep => {\n  const step = steps[policyIndex];\n  if (step.op !== \"quote\") {\n    throw new Error(`Policy ${policyIndex} is not a quote step`);\n  }\n  return step;\n};\n```\nThis is clear.\n\nWhat about `policyAdjustmentAmount`:\n```javascript\nconst policyAdjustmentAmount = (\n  baseTotal: number,\n  customerYears: number,\n  isFollowUp: boolean\n): number => {\n  const loyaltyDiscount =\n    customerYears >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD\n      ? LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE * baseTotal\n      : 0;\n\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE * baseTotal;\n  const followUpDiscount = isFollowUp ? FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE * baseTotal : 0;\n\n  return firstInsuranceSurcharge - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount;\n};\n```\nThis is clear. Could simplify the conditional with `surchargeWhen` helper? Like:\n```\nconst followUpDiscount = surchargeWhen(isFollowUp, baseTotal, FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE);\nconst loyaltyDiscount = surchargeWhen(customerYears >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD, baseTotal, LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE);\n```\nThis removes duplication of conditional pattern! And uses the existing `surchargeWhen` helper. That's a good Rule 3 refactoring.\n\nActually, that's a nice improvement. `policyAdjustmentAmount` has a ternary that's conceptually the same as `surchargeWhen`. We can reuse it.\n\nLet me check:\n- `loyaltyDiscount = surchargeWhen(customerYears >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD, baseTotal, LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE)` ✓\n- `followUpDiscount = surchargeWhen(isFollowUp, baseTotal, FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE)` ✓\n\nThis reduces duplication and reuses the existing helper. Good.\n\nWhat else?\n- In `surchargeForItem`, we use `surchargeWhen` already. Good.\n- In `processScenario`, extract `isFollowUpStep` or just add explaining variable.\n\nI think the best refactoring is:\n1. Use `surchargeWhen` in `policyAdjustmentAmount` to remove conceptual duplication.\n2. Rename `amountAfterClauses` to `damageAfterEnchantmentReduction` to better reveal intent.\n3. Add an explaining variable in `processScenario` for `isFollowUp`.\n\nLet me also think about `basePremiumForGroup`:\n```javascript\nconst basePremiumForGroup = (type: string, count: number): number =>\n  isComponent(type) && count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE\n    ? COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM\n    : count * basePremiumForItem(type);\n```\nCould use `surchargeWhen`? No, because it's selecting between two values, not adding a surcharge. It's fine.\n\nLet me now calculate APP mass. This is a bit tedious. Let me do a rough calculation focusing on the function I'll change.\n\nActually, APP mass is supposed to be calculated for the whole code or the relevant function? The instructions say \"Calculate APP mass before\" and \"Calculate new APP mass\". I think I should calculate for the implementation file overall, or at least for the changed functions. Let me calculate for the entire file to be comprehensive.\n\nActually, calculating APP mass for the entire file with all those constants and object literals is very tedious and error-prone. The instructions say to calculate mass, but it doesn't specify scope. I'll calculate mass for the functions I modify: `policyAdjustmentAmount`, `reimbursementForDamage`, and `processScenario`.\n\nWait, but the example in the instructions calculates mass for a single function. So I'll do the same for the functions I change. Or maybe just one function. Let me pick `policyAdjustmentAmount` as the main refactoring target.\n\nCurrent `policyAdjustmentAmount`:\n```javascript\nconst policyAdjustmentAmount = (\n  baseTotal: number,\n  customerYears: number,\n  isFollowUp: boolean\n): number => {\n  const loyaltyDiscount =\n    customerYears >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD\n      ? LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE * baseTotal\n      : 0;\n\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE * baseTotal;\n  const followUpDiscount = isFollowUp ? FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE * baseTotal : 0;\n\n  return firstInsuranceSurchARGE_RATE * baseTotal; // wait let me re-read\n};\n```\n\nLet me re-read exact code:\n```\nconst policyAdjustmentAmount = (\n  baseTotal: number,\n  customerYears: number,\n  isFollowUp: boolean\n): number => {\n  const loyaltyDiscount =\n    customerYears >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD\n      ? LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE * baseTotal\n      : 0;\n\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE * baseTotal;\n  const followUpDiscount = isFollowUp ? FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE * baseTotal : 0;\n\n  return firstInsuranceSurcharge - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount;\n};\n```\n\nMass for current `policyAdjustmentAmount`:\n- Constants: 1 (the literal 0 in else branch) = 1\n- Bindings: 4 (baseTotal, customerYears, isFollowUp, loyaltyDiscount, firstInsuranceSurcharge, followUpDiscount) wait that's 6. Also the parameter bindings and variable bindings. = 6\n- Invocations: 2 (* for LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE * baseTotal, * for FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE * baseTotal, * for FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE * baseTotal) = 6. Also subtraction operations? The instructions mention `+` in the example as invocation? Actually in the example `sum + num` was counted as invocation (2). But that's the `+` operator. So `-` operations are also invocations. There are 2 subtractions. Total invocations = 6 + 4 = 10? Wait, binary operators might not be \"invocations\" in the strict sense. The example says \"Invocations: 2 (reduce, +)\" which is weird because `+` isn't a function call. The example treated `+` as an invocation. So I'll treat binary operators as invocations too. So * operations = 3, - operations = 2, total = 5 invocations. But wait, the ternary `?:` is a conditional. And the `>=` and `===` are comparisons... are those invocations? In the example, they didn't count `>=`. Hmm.\n\nActually, the APP definition says:\n- Constant (Mass: 1): Literal values\n- Binding/Scalar (Mass: 1): Variables, parameters\n- Invocation (Mass: 2): Function calls (calculate(), Math.max())\n- Conditional (Mass: 4): Control flow (if, switch, ?:)\n- Loop (Mass: 5): Iteration\n- Assignment (Mass: 6): Mutations\n\nSo binary operators like `+`, `-`, `*`, `>=`, `===` are not explicitly counted. The example is a bit confusing: \"Invocations: 2 (reduce, +)\". They counted `reduce` as invocation and `+` as invocation? Or maybe they meant `reduce` is the invocation and `+` is an operation but counted as invocation? This is ambiguous. \n\nI think the safest interpretation is: invocations are function/method calls. Binary operators are NOT invocations. The example might be using a loose interpretation. Let me follow the strict definition.\n\nSo current `policyAdjustmentAmount` mass:\n- Constants: 1 (literal 0) = 1\n- Bindings: 6 (baseTotal, customerYears, isFollowUp, loyaltyDiscount, firstInsuranceSurcharge, followUpDiscount) = 6\n- Invocations: 0 (no function calls) = 0\n- Conditionals: 2 (two ternary `?:`) = 8\n- Loops: 0 = 0\n- Assignments: 0 (const declarations are bindings, not assignments) = 0\n\nWait, are const declarations assignments? The definition says \"Assignment (Mass: 6): Mutations (x = 5, count++)\". Const declarations are initial bindings, not mutations. So they're bindings, not assignments.\n\nTotal mass: 1 + 6 + 0 + 8 + 0 + 0 = 15.\n\nRefactored `policyAdjustmentAmount` using `surchargeWhen`:\n```javascript\nconst policyAdjustmentAmount = (\n  baseTotal: number,\n  customerYears: number,\n  isFollowUp: boolean\n): number => {\n  const loyaltyDiscount = surchargeWhen(\n    customerYears >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD,\n    baseTotal,\n    LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE\n  );\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE * baseTotal;\n  const followUpDiscount = surchargeWhen(isFollowUp, baseTotal, FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE);\n\n  return firstInsuranceSurcharge - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount;\n};\n```\n\nMass:\n- Constants: 0 = 0\n- Bindings: 6 = 6\n- Invocations: 2 (surchargeWhen called twice) = 4\n- Conditionals: 0 (the conditionals are inside surchargeWhen, but we're calculating this function's mass. The `>=` is a comparison, not a conditional control flow. `surchargeWhen` itself contains a conditional but that's in a different function.) = 0\n- Loops: 0 = 0\n- Assignments: 0 = 0\n\nWait, but `surchargeWhen` contains a conditional `?:`. If we're counting the function's own control flow, `surchargeWhen` invocations don't add conditionals to this function. But the total program mass would include surchargeWhen's conditional. Since we're comparing the same helper exists in both cases, it's a wash.\n\nTotal mass for this function: 0 + 6 + 4 + 0 + 0 + 0 = 10.\n\nMass reduction: 15 -> 10. Good.\n\nBut wait, I need to also calculate mass for `surchargeWhen` itself? It exists in both cases so it cancels out.\n\nNow, should I also include the `>=` comparison? The conditional operator `?:` is control flow. The `>=` is a comparison operator, not control flow. So no conditional mass for it.\n\nOK, that's a nice improvement. But actually, we removed conditionals by pushing them into `surchargeWhen`. This might be considered just moving mass around, not reducing total program mass. However, it removes conceptual duplication (the pattern of \"if condition then rate * base else 0\" appears in `surchargeForItem` too). So Rule 3 (No Duplication) is satisfied.\n\nLet me also consider the renaming in `reimbursementForDamage`.\n\nCurrent:\n```javascript\nconst reimbursementForDamage = (policyItem: Item, damageAmount: number): number => {\n  const enchantment = typeof policyItem.enchantment === \"number\" ? policyItem.enchantment : 0;\n  const amountAfterClauses =\n    enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD\n      ? damageAmount * HIGH_