rmal logic.\n  \n  Wait, let me look at \"should apply enchantment clause over dragon material when both apply\":\n  - dragon material sword, enchantment 9\n  - damage 1000\n  - enchantment >= 9: 1000 * 0.5 = 500\n  - 500 - 100 = 400\n  - payout 400, remainingCap 1600\n  - Cap: 2 * 1000 = 2000. 2000 - 400 = 1600. Matches.\n  \n  \"should apply dragon-material clause when only it applies\":\n  - dragon material, enchantment 5\n  - damage 800\n  - enchantment < 9, so 800\n  - 800 - 100 = 700\n  - payout 700, remainingCap 1300\n  - Cap: 2 * 1000 = 2000. 2000 - 700 = 1300. Matches.\n  \n  So the dragon material clause doesn't actually add any special behavior beyond normal deductible? That seems odd but the tests pass. Maybe the dragon material is just to test interaction. The implementation doesn't have explicit dragon logic but tests pass.\n\n  Hmm, wait. The test says \"should fully reimburse dragon-material damage minus deductible\". If dragon material had no deductible normally, then minus deductible means there IS a deductible. So maybe dragon material normally has no deductible but when combined with enchantment... no, the implementation treats all the same.\n\n  Actually, looking again, maybe the dragon clause is supposed to skip the deductible entirely (full reimbursement), and the test is checking 800 damage => 800 payout. But expected is 700. So that's not it.\n\n  Or maybe dragon clause doubles the insured value? No, cap is 2 * 1000 = 2000, which is already the multiplier.\n\n  So the current implementation passes tests. The dragon clause might just be descriptive text. The implementation is correct for the tests.\n\n  Actually, wait - maybe I'm missing something. Let me re-read more carefully. The function `reimbursementForDamage` doesn't use `material` at all. But `policyItem.material` exists. The dragon material tests pass without it because the numbers work out. So either:\n  1. The dragon material clause is implicitly already handled by the cap/existing