cessClaim`:\n  ```ts\n  const processClaim = (...) => {\n    const payoutBeforeCap = damages.reduce((sum, damage) => {\n      if (damage.amount < 0) throw error;\n      const policyItem = policyItems.find(...);\n      if (!policyItem) throw error;\n      return sum + reimbursementForDamage(policyItem, damage.amount);\n    }, 0);\n    const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(payoutBeforeCap, remainingCap));\n    return { payout, remainingCap: Math.floor(remainingCap - payout) };\n  };\n  ```\n\n  Could be:\n  ```ts\n  const reimbursementForDamageEntry = (policyItems: Item[], damage: {itemType: string; amount: number}): number => {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) throw ...;\n    const policyItem = policyItems.find(...);\n    if (!policyItem) throw ...;\n    return reimbursementForDamage(policyItem, damage.amount);\n  };\n\n  const processClaim = (...) => {\n    const payoutBeforeCap = damages.reduce((sum, damage) => sum + reimbursementForDamageEntry(policyItems, damage), 0);\n    const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(payoutBeforeCap, remainingCap));\n    return { payout, remainingCap: Math.floor(remainingCap - payout) };\n  };\n  ```\n\n  This reduces duplication? No, it extracts nested logic for clarity (Rule 2). It adds one function, one binding, maybe increases mass slightly. But it improves clarity. Since Rule 2 > APP, that's acceptable.\n\n  Potential refactor 3: In `processScenario`, the claim handling logic is inline. Could extract `handleClaimStep` to make processScenario more readable. But might be fine.\n\n  Potential refactor 4: `quoteForItems` has `isFollowUp` passed in. In `processScenario`, `isFollowUp = index > 0`. This is correct for the test \"should apply follow-up contract discount on the second quote\". But is it correct for a scenario where step 0 is claim and step 1 is quote? The test only has quotes. The spec says \"follow-up contract\" which means any quote after the first. `index > 0` works for any step after the first, even if step 0 was a claim. Is that 