putes a quote premium. Good.\n  - `basePremiumTotal` - total base premium for items. Good.\n  - `policyAdjustmentAmount` - the net adjustment amount. Good.\n  - `insuranceSumForItems` - sum of insured values. Good.\n  - `surchargeForItem` - surcharge for an item. Good.\n  - `basePremiumForGroup` - premium for a group of same-type items. Good.\n  - `basePremiumForItem` - base premium for one item. Good.\n  - `insuredValueFor` - insured value for item type. Good.\n  - `lookupValue` - looks up value in table. Good.\n  - `conditionalAdjustment` - conditional adjustment. Could be better.\n\n  I'll do these refactorings:\n  1. Extract `reimbursementForDamageEntry` helper from `processClaim` to clarify the per-damage lookup and validation logic.\n  2. Rename `conditionalAdjustment` to `rateAdjustmentIf` to better reveal intent.\n  3. Rename `quoteStepForPolicy` to `requireQuoteStepAt` to better reveal intent.\n\n  But I should make one change at a time and run tests. Let me start with extracting the helper, which is the biggest clarity win.\n\n  Actually, I need to be careful about the APP mass calculation. Let me think about what to measure. Since the instructions require before/after mass, I should do it for the whole file or for the specific function changed. I'll do the whole file.\n\n  Let me count mass for current file:\n\n  Constants (literal values, Mass 1 each):\n  - 5, 3, 60, 0.5, 0.3, 5, 8, 0.5, 0.2, 2, 0.1, 0.15, 100, 2 (top-level) = 14\n  - \"sword\", \"amulet\", \"staff\", \"potion\", \"rune\", \"moonstone\" in BASE_PREMIUMS = 6\n  - 100, 60, 80, 40, 25, 25 values in BASE_PREMIUMS = 6\n  - \"sword\", \"amulet\", \"staff\", \"potion\", \"rune\", \"moonstone\" in INSURANCE_VALUES = 6\n  - 1000, 600, 800, 400, 250, 250 values = 6\n  - 0 in `item.cursed ?? false`? No, `false` is a constant. `0` in `?? 0` in `basePremiumTotal`. `0` initial value in reduces.\n  \n  This is getting very tedious. Maybe I should count for a specific function or do a rough count. The i